.TH NOG 1 "October 2026" "nog v2.1.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
nog \- tier-aware package manager for KognogOS
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nog
.I <command>
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ packages ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nog
is the package manager for KognogOS. It wraps
.BR pacman (8)
\(em and optionally an AUR helper such as
.BR yay (8)
or
.BR paru (8)
\(em with a three-tier update management system, giving every package on the
system a protection level based on how critical it is.
.PP
Tier 1 packages (the kernel, bootloader, glibc, systemd, mesa) are held for
30 days after upstream publish before auto-releasing. Tier 2 packages are
held for 15 days. Tier 3 packages are held for 7 days. Expert users can
switch Tier 1 to a manual-sign-off mode via
.B tier-pins.toml
if they prefer to eyeball every kernel upgrade personally. See
.B TIER SYSTEM
for the full model.
.PP
Since v2.0.0 (the v2 arc complete), one command set covers every source \(em
the repositories, the AUR, Flatpak and Snap: \fBinstall\fR, \fBremove\fR,
\fBreinstall\fR, \fBsearch\fR, \fBlist\fR, \fBinfo\fR, \fBtier\fR,
\fBlock\fR/\fBunlock\fR, \fBstatus\fR, \fBhistory\fR, \fBclean\fR \(em and
every query command answers in JSON with \fB\-\-json\fR, for programs such as
nogForge.
.PP
.B nog
should always be run as your regular user. It escalates to root only where
genuinely required — by invoking
.B sudo pacman
for package transactions and
.B sudo tee
for writing
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
and
.IR /etc/nog/locks.toml .
See the
.B PRIVILEGES AND SUDO
section below for the complete privilege model.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B nog install \fR[\fB--yes\fR] \fIpackage\fR [\fIpackage\fR...] | \fIfile\fR.pkg.tar.* [\fIfile\fR...]
Install one or more packages. Tier classification is printed as informational
output; installs are never blocked or gated — explicit user commands execute
user intent. If an AUR helper is configured (see
.BR nog.conf (5)),
the install routes through the helper, so AUR-only packages work transparently
without any pre-check. Tier protection applies to future updates, not to installs.
Since v1.7.0 a name can carry its source: \fBaur/\fIname\fR is always built from
the AUR (never swapped for a repository package that provides the same name), and
\fIrepo\fB/\fIname\fR comes from that repository only. nogForge installs this way.
.IP
\fBThe install chain (v1.9.0, C3).\fR A name is looked for in this order:
the repositories (chaotic-aur included), then the AUR, then Flatpak, then Snap
\(em each source only when the one before does not have it. The table shows
which source answered before anything is downloaded or built. A Flatpak is
matched exactly, never fuzzily: the full app ID (\fBorg.gimp.GIMP\fR), the ID's
last word (\fBgimp\fR), or the app's name (\fBspotify\fR for
\fBcom.spotify.Client\fR), case ignored; when several apps answer to one name
nog numbers them (a snap of that exact name too) and asks which one; with
\fB--yes\fR or no terminal it lists them and stops. \fBflatpak/\fIapp-id\fR names one
directly. A snap is
matched by its exact store name. \fBflatpak/\fIname\fR and \fBsnap/\fIname\fR
look in that source only; a source switched off with \fBnog deactivate\fR is
skipped and the not-found notice says so.
.IP
Flatpak and snapd are never required (\fIdocs/v2-design.md\fR, ruling 1).
When the chain reaches a backend that is not set up and a person is at the
keyboard, nog offers to set it up, once, with the default \fBno\fR: Flatpak is
the \fBflatpak\fR package from \fIextra\fR (it comes with Flathub); Snap is
\fBsnapd\fR built from the AUR by the helper, \fBsnapd.socket\fR started, and
\fB/snap\fR linked for classic snaps. Without a person, or with \fB--yes\fR, a
missing backend is only named.
.IP
Each source makes its own transaction, in the chain's order: pacman (with the
AUR through the helper), then \fBflatpak install\fR (it shows the runtimes an
app needs and asks), then \fBsudo snap install\fR, which never asks \(em so
nog asks \fBInstall these? [Y/n]\fR itself when a snap is involved, as it does
for the AUR. A snap the store marks \fIclassic\fR (it may change anything on
the system, like a package) is said so in the table and installed with
\fB--classic\fR. A step that stops ends the run, and the notice says what had
already gone in. Flatpaks and snaps are Tier 3 unless pinned, by app ID or snap
name, as in \fBnog update\fR.
.IP
\fB--yes\fR (v1.9.0, issue #32) answers the install's question for you:
\fBpacman --noconfirm\fR, \fBflatpak install -y\fR, and nog's own question
for Snap; package files too. Never the AUR: the helper stops to let you review
each build recipe, and that review is the AUR's one safety, so an AUR name with
\fB--yes\fR stops before anything. With \fB--yes\fR repository packages go to
pacman directly, not through the helper (there is nothing for it to ask).
Without \fB--yes\fR and with no terminal on standard input, nog stops first and
says that nothing is at the keyboard to answer \(em pacman would read
end-of-input as "no", and the old message blamed you for it.
.IP
Since v1.5.8 pacman asks before installing (no \fB--noconfirm\fR), showing
exactly what will be installed, dependencies included.
.IP
Since v1.8.0 nog first shows where each package comes from, before anything is
downloaded or built: package, version, source and tier. The source follows
pacman's own order \(em the exact name in a repository, then a repository
package that provides the name (said plainly), then a group \(em and then the
AUR, built by the helper. A name found nowhere stops the run here. When the AUR
is involved nog asks \fBInstall these? [Y/n]\fR itself, because pacman's
question would only come after the build; then a line says who takes over
(pacman, or the helper with its recipe review and build).
.IP
Since v1.5.0 a locally built package file \(em any argument containing
.B .pkg.tar
\(em is installed with
.BR "pacman -U" ,
for example the output of
.BR makepkg (8).
Its name, version and tier are read from the file itself, since a local build
has no repository entry. Names and files cannot be mixed in one command; they
are separate pacman operations, and splitting one request could leave half of
it installed. A mistyped path is reported as a missing file, never searched for
in the repositories. There is no signature gate beyond pacman's own
.BR LocalFileSigLevel .
.IP
Since v1.6.0 \fBnog install\fR installs only what was asked, like
\fBpacman -S\fR: it never updates the system first (v1.5.6 to v1.5.8 did, when
the package lists were more than a day old). It never refreshes the lists on
their own either, which would be a partial upgrade. If an install fails and the
lists are old (nog's record of the computer's last completed update,
\fI/var/lib/nog/last-update\fR since v2.1.0, is missing, over a day old, or older
than the lists), nog says so: \fBnog update\fR, then try again.
.IP
Since v1.5.4, when standard input is not a terminal and a requested name is not
in any enabled repository (so the helper would build it from the AUR), nog
refuses before handing off and says why: the helper stops to let you review the
build recipe, and nothing is at the keyboard to answer. Answering for you would
skip that review, so nog does not.
.TP
.B nog clean \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIa\fR,\fIb\fR]
Clean up what every source leaves behind (v2.0.0, C5). One report, a numbered
row per category with its size: \fBcache\fR \(em old downloads in pacman's
cache (every \fBCacheDir\fR in
.IR pacman.conf ;
since v1.5.5, keeping 3 versions of each Tier 1 package, 2 of Tier 2, 1 of
Tier 3 \(em \fBtier1_keep\fR, \fBtier2_keep\fR, \fBtier3_keep\fR under
\fB[clean]\fR in
.IR nog.conf
\(em never the installed version, never a newer download, plus every file of a
package no longer installed and leftover \fBdownload-XXXXXX\fR folders);
\fBorphans\fR \(em packages installed as dependencies that nothing needs any
more (\fBpacman -Qtdq\fR); \fBaur\fR \(em the AUR helper's build trees
(\fI~/.cache/yay/<pkg>\fR or \fI~/.cache/paru/clone/<pkg>\fR); \fBflatpak\fR
\(em runtimes no installed app uses (nog's estimate; flatpak decides the exact
list); \fBsnap\fR \(em the old revisions snapd keeps after updates. Then
\fB"Clean up which? [all, numbers like 1 3, or n for none]"\fR; nothing is
removed without an answer. Each category is removed by the tool that owns it,
in its own voice: nog removes the cache files (root) and the build trees (your
own files, no password); \fBpacman -Rns\fR shows the orphans and asks;
\fBflatpak uninstall --unused\fR works out the exact list, shows it and removes
it (flatpak does not ask here; your pick at nog's question is the yes); \fBsnap remove
--revision\fR takes each old revision (root). \fB\-\-only\fR picks categories
and still asks \fB[y/N]\fR; \fB\-\-yes\fR answers that and the tools'
questions; \fB\-\-json\fR is the report alone, for nogForge. It refuses while
pacman's lock file exists.
.TP
.B nog remove \fR[\fB--yes\fR] \fIpackage\fR [\fIpackage\fR...]
Remove one or more packages via
.BR pacman (8),
with what only they needed (\fB-Rs\fR). Works uniformly for packages originally
installed from official repos or AUR. Since v1.5.8 pacman shows the list and asks
before removing anything.
.IP
\fBWhat goes along, and who still uses it (v1.9.0, F-13, issue #49).\fR
\fBpacman -Rs\fR takes every dependency nothing else \fIrequires\fR \(em a
package another installed app uses only \fIoptionally\fR counts as unneeded and
goes too, silently (removing Elisa would have taken \fBvlc-plugin-ffmpeg\fR, and
VLC most of its video formats). Before pacman runs, nog reads pacman's own dry
run (\fB-Rsp\fR), finds every package in it that an installed package outside
the removal lists as optional, says so in plain words with pacman's reason
(\fIell goes too, but bluez-utils uses it (optional: for btpclient)\fR), and
asks \fBKeep \fIpackage\fB? [Y/n]\fR for each, default yes. Kept means marked
as installed on purpose (\fBnog keep\fR), so this removal and every later one
leave it alone. With \fB--yes\fR every such package is kept without asking.
.IP
A name that is not an installed package is looked for among the installed
Flatpaks (by app ID, its last word, or the app's name) and snaps, and removed by
its own tool: \fBflatpak uninstall\fR shows what goes and asks; \fBsudo snap
remove\fR never asks, so nog asks \fBRemove \fIname\fB? [Y/n]\fR first (Enter means
yes, as at pacman's and flatpak's question). A name
installed nowhere stops the whole request before anything. \fB--yes\fR answers
pacman's and flatpak's questions and nog's own; without it and with no terminal
on standard input nog stops first, as for an install.
.TP
.B nog keep \fIpackage\fR [\fIpackage\fR...]
Mark installed packages as installed on purpose (v1.9.0, F-13) \(em pacman's
own install reason, \fBpacman -D --asexplicit\fR, without leaving nog for it.
A kept package is never taken along by the removal of something else. This is
what \fBnog remove\fR offers for each package it would take along that another
app still uses. A name that is not installed stops before anything; root is
needed, so sudo asks once.
.TP
.B nog info \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Everything nog knows about one package (v2.0.0): where it comes from, its
version, whether it is installed, its tier and why, how long its updates wait
(or that it is locked), the age of the repository's build, its description and
the details its source gives (pacman's fields, \fBflatpak info\fR, \fBsnap
info\fR). What is installed wins: pacman's database, then an installed Flatpak
(by app ID, its last word or its name) or snap; then the repositories, the
AUR, Flatpak, Snap. A name known nowhere stops with the way to search.
.TP
.B nog reinstall \fR[\fB\-\-yes\fR] \fIpackage\fR [\fIpackage\fR...]
Put back the files of installed packages, each through its own tool (v2.0.0):
pacman reinstalls the same version and asks; the AUR helper rebuilds (never
under \fB\-\-yes\fR: the recipe review needs a person); \fBflatpak install
--reinstall\fR for a Flatpak app. A snap cannot be reinstalled in place \(em
snapd has no such step \(em so nog stops and says the remove-and-install way,
with what it costs (snapd drops the data after a while). A name that is not
installed stops before anything; \fBnog install\fR is for something new.
.TP
.B nog status \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
The dashboard (v2.0.0): what is installed and from where (packages, the ones
you chose, foreign ones, Flatpak apps, snaps), which sources are on, the update
plan's buckets \(em ready now, held by their tier, to be asked about \(em with
what is released next, the locks, the last run, the last update and the last
reboot advice. The plan comes from the same code as \fBnog update --json\fR,
stopped before any question or change.
.TP
.B nog history \fR[\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
What nog has done (v2.0.0), newest first, from its own logs under
.IR ~/.local/share/nog/logs/
(kept 90 days): every run with its outcome, every package an update decided
on (source, bucket, old and new version, what happened), and every reboot
advice. Default 30 days.
.TP
.B nog update
Compute a tier-aware system upgrade plan and hand each source to the tool
that owns it \(em
.BR pacman (8)
for the official repositories, then the configured AUR helper, then
.BR flatpak (1)
and
.BR snap (8).
Every pending upgrade is shown before any transaction starts. Since v1.5.4 a
.B SUMMARY
table comes first: one row per official repository in
.I pacman.conf
order, then AUR, Flatpak and Snap, each with its Ready, Held and total counts
(plus an \fBAsk you\fR column when anything is Unknown). A source whose query
failed reads \fBcould not check\fR, never 0; one switched off with
.B nog deactivate
reads \fBoff\fR; one that is not installed has no row. Every table row carries
a \fBSource\fR column with the same word. Updates are classified into
one of three buckets:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B Ready to install
\(em hold window has expired; safe to upgrade
.IP \(bu 2
.B Held
\(em still within the tier's hold window, the newest build still inside its
safety wait, or Tier 1 package with
.B manual_signoff = true
.IP \(bu 2
.B Unknown
\(em no usable build date (locally built, disabled repo, helper lookup
failed, or a sync-DB entry that doesn't match the pending candidate's version)
.RE
Each
.B Unknown
package triggers an interactive
.B "[y/N]"
prompt. Pending official repo upgrades come from
.BR checkupdates (8);
pending AUR upgrades come from
.B "<helper> -Qua" .
Build dates come from the fresh sync-database snapshot
.BR checkupdates (8)
itself just synced (its private dbpath: \fB$CHECKUPDATES_DB\fR, default
\fB${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/checkup-db-<uid>/\fR) \(em the same snapshot that produced
the candidate list \(em and, for AUR packages not found in any sync DB, from
the helper's cached metadata (\fB<helper> -Sai\fR). If the snapshot is
missing, nog falls back to \fB/var/lib/pacman/sync\fR with a warning; holds
may then be dated from stale build dates. A hold is only ever dated from a DB
entry matching the pending candidate's exact version \(em mismatches bucket
as Unknown.
The handoff runs in four steps, skipping any source with nothing cleared.
.B pacman -Syu
upgrades the official repositories with
.B --ignore=
listing all held and user-skipped packages; the helper is then invoked as
.B "<helper> -S"
with the cleared AUR package names only, so a held AUR package is never named
and cannot move. Flatpak and snap follow, unchanged.
.PP
Failure handling differs by step. If
.B pacman
fails, nog cancels without touching another source \(em AUR packages are
compiled against official libraries and must not be built on a system whose
repository upgrade did not complete. If a later step fails, nog reports it and
asks whether to continue, defaulting to
.BR no ;
a non-interactive run stops.
.PP
Every run is appended to a per-day CSV log
.RB ( "YYYYMMDD nog-update.csv"
under
.BR "[paths] run_logs" ,
default
.IR ~/.local/share/nog/logs )
with one row per pending package. Since v1.4.3 each row records its own
.B source
.RB ( pacman ,
.BR aur ,
.BR flatpak ,
.BR snap )
and its own
.B outcome
\(em
.B held
for a package nog held,
.B skipped
for an unknown you declined, and otherwise the result of that package's own
source step:
.BR installed ,
.BR "did not complete (status N)" ,
.B cancelled
(you said no at nog's own prompt) or
.B not run
(an earlier step stopped the run). When a step does not complete, the
.B detail
column keeps the line that explains it: the tool's last
.B error:
line, or the question that was declined. The tool's own output still reaches
the terminal live; nog keeps a copy of the end of its error stream, nothing
more. Logs older than 90 days are pruned automatically. Logging soft-fails with
a warning and never blocks an update.
.PP
Before the buckets render,
.B nog update
checks installed kernel and
.IR <kernel>-headers
versions for every Tier 1 kernel. If any pair disagrees
(e.g.
.IR linux-lts
at 6.18.22-1 but
.IR linux-lts-headers
at 6.18.29-1, the fingerprint of a v1.0.2-style desync), a red warning block
is printed naming each desynced pair and pointing at
.BR --realign .
.TP
.B nog update \-\-realign
Recover from a kernel / headers version mismatch. For each desynced kernel
whose pending upgrade version matches the installed headers version, the row
is pulled from the
.B Held
bucket into the
.B Ready
bucket with the annotation
.RB [ "Tier 1 · realigned to match installed headers" ].
The subsequent transaction upgrades the kernel to match the headers in a
single coherent step, after which the next DKMS rebuild succeeds. If no held
kernel matches the installed headers version, a clean fall-through notice is
printed and
.B nog update
proceeds with the standard plan.
.TP
.B nog update \-\-yes
For a script, a timer or the installer (v2.1.0, #56): nog answers for you.
The handoff question is answered yes and pacman gets \fB\-\-noconfirm\fR, so
the Ready packages go in. \fBUnknown\fR packages are kept back and listed: each
has no usable build date, so updating it is a decision, and \fB\-\-yes\fR never
makes it. With nobody at the keyboard, the AUR's build review waits for a
person: the AUR packages are kept back, still Ready, with a hint. When a later
step fails, \fB\-\-yes\fR takes the question's default and stops there.
With nobody at the keyboard and sudo asking for a password nobody can type,
nog says so before anything changes (exit status 1); a sudo rule that lets the
user run \fBpacman\fR without a password, or \fBNOG_ASKPASS=1\fR, makes it work.
Without \fB\-\-yes\fR and with nobody at the keyboard, nog says that is the
cause before anything changes, instead of reading end-of-input as "no".
.TP
.B nog search \fIquery\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Search every source (v2.0.0): the repositories in pacman's order, the AUR
through the helper when that source is on, then Flatpak (\fBflatpak search\fR)
and Snap (\fBsnap find\fR) when they are on and installed. Results come per
source, each with its tier (red Tier 1, yellow Tier 2, green Tier 3); a
Flatpak row says the exact way to install it (\fBflatpak/\fIapp-id\fR), a
snap row \fBsnap/\fIname\fR and whether it is classic. A long list is cut at
30 per source on screen (\fB\-\-json\fR has them all); a source that could
not be reached is said, never shown as nothing.
.TP
.B nog pin \fIpackage\fR \fB--tier=\fR\fI<1|2|3>\fR
Pin a package to a specific tier. The change is written to
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
via
.B sudo tee
and survives reboots. Pinning to Tier 3 removes any existing Tier 1 or Tier 2
entry for that package (Tier 3 is the implicit default for anything not listed).
Since v2.0.0, \fBnog tier \fIpackage\fB --set \fIN\fR does the same and says
what changes.
.TP
.B nog tier \fIpackage\fR [\fB\-\-set\fR \fI1|2|3\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
A package's tier and where it comes from, in words (v2.0.0): pinned in
.IR tier-pins.toml ,
the headers of a pinned kernel, the 32-bit half of a pinned package, built from
the same recipe as a pinned sibling, a member of a \fB[groups]\fR entry, or the
default. With the days its updates wait, and whether it needs a sign-off.
\fB\-\-set\fR moves it (root: \fBsudo tee\fR); already there means nothing
changes.
.TP
.B nog lock \fR[\fIpackage\fR...] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Lock installed packages at their installed version (v2.0.0): a locked package
is never updated until it is unlocked. \fBnog update\fR lists it as held,
\fB"locked by you"\fR, passes it to pacman as \fB--ignore\fR, and
\fB\-\-promote\fR never pulls it in. Written to
.IR /etc/nog/locks.toml
(root: \fBsudo tee\fR), so every user of the computer and nogForge see the
same locks; a name that is not installed stops before anything. Alone,
\fBnog lock\fR lists the locks.
.TP
.B nog unlock \fIpackage\fR [\fIpackage\fR...] [\fB--promote\fR]
Remove a lock set with \fBnog lock\fR (v2.0.0): the package is offered again
by \fBnog update\fR by its tier's rules. A package that is not locked is said
so, with its tier and why, and nothing changes. With
.BR --promote ,
also force-upgrade the package immediately via
.BR pacman (8)
or the configured helper, bypassing the hold window and any sign-off policy
(its meaning since v1.0.4). This is the escape hatch for a held Tier 1 package.
.TP
.B nog deactivate \fIsource\fR
Source kill switch (v1.0.9; \fBflatpak\fR v1.1.0, \fBsnap\fR v1.2.0). Valid
sources:
.BR aur ,
.BR chaotic-aur ,
.BR flatpak ,
and
.BR snap .
Deactivating
.B flatpak
persists a flag in
.I /etc/nog/sources.toml
so
.B nog update
neither queries nor applies flatpak updates; installed apps stay installed
but frozen. The flatpak binary itself is never touched.
Deactivating
.B aur
persists a flag in
.I /etc/nog/sources.toml
and turns off every AUR-aware path at once — update detection, install
routing, and the upgrade handoff (which then runs through
.BR pacman (8)
only, so foreign packages cannot move). The configured helper in
.I nog.conf
is left untouched and resumes on activation. Deactivating
.B chaotic-aur
comments the \fB[chaotic-aur]\fR section out of
.I /etc/pacman.conf
(timestamped backup first, marker \fB#nog#\fR), then refreshes the sync
databases: no tool on the system can resolve from the repository until it is
reactivated, and installed chaotic packages stay installed but frozen.
Intended for incident response — e.g. an active AUR supply-chain attack.
.TP
.B nog activate \fIsource\fR
Reverse of
.BR "nog deactivate" .
Restores the AUR paths, or uncomments exactly the pacman.conf lines nog
disabled (hand-written comments survive byte-for-byte) and refreshes the
databases.
.
.SH NOG FOR PROGRAMS (v1.6.0)
nogForge and other programs read nog's decisions as data. With
.BR \-\-json ,
stdout carries exactly one JSON document and everything nog usually prints goes
to stderr; no banner is printed and no line is written to the run history.
.TP
.B nog list \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Installed packages with their tier and source. With \fB\-\-json\fR, also
whether each was chosen or pulled in, what needs it, and why it may not be
removed (Tier 1, the \fBbase\fR set, or needed by another package).
.TP
.B nog search \-\-json \fIquery\fR
Every source (v2.0.0): the repositories, the AUR through the helper when that
source is on, Flatpak and Snap when on and installed. A Flatpak or snap row
carries \fBinstall_as\fR (\fBflatpak/\fIapp-id\fR, \fBsnap/\fIname\fR), the
exact name to give \fBnog install\fR; \fBsources\fR says what was checked and
any problem.
.TP
.B nog list \-\-json
Since v2.0.0 the installed Flatpak apps (\fBsource\fR \fBflatpak\fR, with
\fBremote\fR and \fBruntime\fR) and snaps (\fBsource\fR \fBsnap\fR, with
\fBrevision\fR, \fBtracking\fR, \fBpublisher\fR, \fBbase\fR) are rows too, each
with \fBremove_as\fR, the name \fBnog remove\fR takes.
.TP
.B nog info \fIname\fR \-\-json
One package: \fBfound\fR, \fBsource\fR, \fBinstalled\fR, \fBversion\fR,
\fBdescription\fR, \fBtier\fR and \fBtier_reason\fR, \fBhold\fR in words,
\fBlocked\fR, \fBbuilt_days_ago\fR, and \fBfields\fR (key/value pairs from the
source's own tool).
.TP
.B nog tier \fIname\fR \-\-json
\fBtier\fR, \fBreason\fR, \fBhold_days\fR, \fBmanual_signoff\fR.
.TP
.B nog lock \-\-json
\fBlocked\fR: the lock list. (A locked package also shows in the plan's held
rows with \fBlocked\fR true and no \fBready_on\fR.)
.TP
.B nog status \-\-json
\fBinstalled\fR counts, \fBsources\fR, the \fBplan\fR (as \fBnog update
--json\fR gives it), \fBcounts\fR, \fBlocked\fR, \fBlast_run\fR,
\fBlast_update\fR, \fBlast_reboot_advice\fR.
.TP
.B nog history \-\-json \fR[\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR]
\fBruns\fR, \fBupdates\fR and \fBreboots\fR: the CSV rows as objects, newest
first.
.TP
.B nog clean \-\-json
The maintenance report alone, nothing removed: \fBcategories\fR (\fBid\fR,
\fBwhat\fR, \fBitems\fR, \fBbytes\fR, \fBnames\fR, \fBhow\fR), \fBtotal_bytes\fR
and the cache's totals. A program then runs \fBnog clean --only \fIa\fB,\fIb\fR
(nog and the tools ask in its window) or adds \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
.B nog update \-\-json \fR[\fB\-\-keep\fR \fIa\fR,\fIb\fR]
The update plan, computed by the same code as a real update and stopped before
any question or transaction: ready, held (with \fBready_on\fR,
\fBkept_back\fR and \fBcoupled_to\fR), unknown, the sources, and each
tier's hold days.
.TP
.B nog update \fIa b c\fR
Update only the packages named (v1.6.1): one table, \fBUPDATING ONLY WHAT YOU
NAMED\fR, and nothing else moves. Everything else that is ready is kept back
this time, so the coupling rules still say what must move together, and pacman
lists each package it skips; nog says so first. A named package that is on hold
and not promoted, waits on a partner, has no update, or is also kept back stops
the run before any question, one line each with what to do (exit status 1).
With \fB\-\-promote\fR, a held one named in both goes in.
.TP
.B nog update \-\-promote \fIa\fR,\fIb\fR
Make these held packages ready now; they install with the rest. A partner the
coupling rules say must move with one is promoted too ("promoted with ...").
\fB\-\-keep\fR wins over \fB\-\-promote\fR.
.TP
.B nog update \-\-keep \fIa\fR,\fIb\fR
Keep these packages back this time. They are held before the coupling rules
run, so what must move with them stays back too.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command did what was asked (for \fBnog update\fR: every step that ran
completed; packages held, kept back or left Unknown are not a failure).
.TP
.B 1
The command stopped, or an error ended it; the reason is printed. This
includes a question answered no, and nobody at the keyboard to answer one.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was not understood (a mistyped option or command).
.TP
.B 3
\fBnog update\fR ran, and a later step (the AUR, Flatpak, Snap) did not
complete (v2.1.0). The summary names each one and why when nog knows it; the
day's runs log says \fBincomplete\fR. Run \fBnog update\fR again to finish them.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOG_ASKPASS=1
Every \fBsudo\fR nog runs gets \fB\-A\fR, and the AUR helper gets
\fB\-\-sudoflags \-A\fR: the program named by \fBSUDO_ASKPASS\fR (a
password window, or since nogForge 1.1 a helper that asks inside nogForge) asks
for the password instead of the terminal. Set by nogForge.
.TP
.B NOG_EVENTS=\fIfile\fR
(v1.7.0) nog appends its steps to \fIfile\fR, one JSON line each: the steps that
will run (\fB{"ev":"steps",...}\fR), each step's start and end (check, keys, pacman,
aur, flatpak, snap; install, remove, clean), nog's own questions
(\fB{"ev":"ask",...}\fR) and the end status. Best-effort: a file that cannot be
written never stops a run. nogForge reads it for its steps view.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-yes
On \fBnog install\fR and \fBnog remove\fR (v1.9.0): answer the question for
you \(em pacman's, flatpak's, nog's own for Snap \(em and keep what another
app still uses on a removal. Never the AUR's build review. For scripts and
programs; see the commands.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help information.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version information.
.
.SH FLATPAK SUPPORT
Since v1.1.0
.B nog update
also reports and applies Flatpak app updates, under the same tier hold
windows as everything else. The hold clock for a flatpak ref is the
publish date of the pending remote commit (from
.BR "flatpak remote-info" ),
the analogue of a package's build date. Flatpak refs classify as Tier 3 by
default and can be pinned like any package with
.BR "nog pin" .
Rows carry a
.B "\(bu flatpak"
marker in the Note column so the source of every update is visible before
you confirm.
.PP
Flatpak is an optional backend: if the
.B flatpak
binary is absent the source is simply dormant, never an error. Because
flatpak has no
.B \-\-ignore
option, nog enforces holds by naming: it passes exactly the refs it cleared
this run and nothing else. A failed remote query is reported and the run
continues with flatpak untouched \(em never assumed quiet.
.
.SH SNAP SUPPORT
Since v1.2.0
.B nog update
also reports and refreshes Snap packages, under the same tier hold windows.
The hold clock for a snap is the publish date of the pending revision in the
snap's tracked channel, read from
.BR "snap info" .
Rows carry a
.B "\(bu snap"
marker in the Note column.
.PP
snapd is packaged only in the AUR on Arch, so nog never requires it: without
the
.B snap
binary the source is dormant and silent, never an error. Unlike every other
nog operation,
.B "snap refresh"
requires root \(em nog stays unprivileged and escalates through
.BR sudo (8)
for the snap steps alone.
.PP
Snap holds are enforced differently from flatpak, and the difference matters.
snapd runs its own auto\-refresh on its own schedule \(em four times a day by
default \(em so naming only the cleared snaps at refresh time held nothing:
snapd refreshed the rest regardless. Since v1.4.1 nog places the tier window
in snapd itself, with
.BR "snap refresh --hold=<hours>" ,
where it actually binds. A held snap is protected from snapd's timer and from
a blanket
.BR "snap refresh" ,
while a specifically named refresh stays unblocked \(em which is how nog still
releases the snap itself once the window closes. If a hold cannot be placed,
nog says
.B "NOT HELD"
rather than reporting a hold it did not achieve.
.
.SH REBOOT ADVICE
After a successful update,
.B nog
compares the running system against what is now installed and, if they differ,
ends the run with a recommendation. It never reboots or restarts anything.
.PP
Every line states its own basis, and the distinction is deliberate:
.TP
.B verified
.B nog
looked at the running system and observed the difference. The line carries both
versions. Three checks are available:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the running kernel, by whether
.I /usr/lib/modules/<uname \-r>
still exists \(em that directory is named for the running kernel and is removed
when the kernel is replaced, so no version string is ever parsed
.IP \(bu 2
the loaded NVIDIA driver, from
.I /proc/driver/nvidia/version
.IP \(bu 2
the running init system, from
.B systemctl \-\-version
.RE
.TP
.B advice
.B nog
confirmed the package was installed but has no way to ask what is running \(em
.BR glibc ,
.B mkinitcpio
and
.B grub
have no such probe. The packages are named and the notice says plainly that it
is advice rather than a finding.
.PP
.BR mesa ,
.BR xorg\-server ,
.B wayland
and
.B dbus
produce a log-out notice instead of a reboot recommendation.
.I <X>\-headers
packages produce nothing: they are never loaded into a running system and they
accompany every kernel.
.PP
Since v1.4.3 the advice is also written down, to
.B YYYYMMDD nog-reboot.csv
beside the run logs: one row per line of advice with its level
.RB ( IMPORTANT ,
.BR NOTE ),
or a single
.B checked
row when the probes ran and found nothing to say. It is written after the run
log is already on disk, so a probe problem can never cost the main record.
.PP
Nothing is printed when there is nothing to report. A package
.B nog
cleared but pacman did not install produces no notice \(em installed versions are
re-read after the handoff rather than assumed from the request \(em and a driver
whose loaded module already matches is silent.
.PP
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/nog/nog.conf
Main nog configuration file. Defines paths, hold windows (\fB[holds]\fR), and
AUR helper preference (\fB[aur]\fR).
.TP
.I /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
Tier assignments for all managed packages. Edited by
.B nog pin
(written via
.BR "sudo tee" )
or manually by the system administrator.
.B manual_signoff
under
.B [tier1]
controls whether Tier 1 auto-releases (default) or stays wholesale held
(expert mode).
.TP
.I /etc/nog/locks.toml
The locks (v2.0.0): \fB[locked] packages = [...]\fR, written by
.B nog lock
and
.B nog unlock
via \fBsudo tee\fR. A missing file is no locks; an unreadable one is said by
\fBnog update\fR and treated as none (nothing is quietly held or released).
.TP
.I /var/lib/nog/last-update
The record of the computer's last completed update (v2.1.0; before, one per
user): written by
.B nog update
right after pacman's step completes, through \fBsudo -n install\fR while sudo
still remembers the password, so it never asks. Where sudo may run pacman only,
nog writes \fI~/.local/share/nog/last-update\fR instead; the newer of the two
counts. Read when an install fails, to say whether the package lists are old.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pacman/sync/*.db
Pacman sync databases. Read-only by nog, for build-date lookup during
.BR "nog update" .
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/nog/holds.tsv
The hold record (v1.5.2): one line per pending update \(em source, name,
installed version, first sighting (Unix time) and every new version seen since.
Written by
.B nog update
as the invoking user; path set by \fBhold_record\fR under \fB[paths]\fR. Holds
that began before the record existed are dated from the run logs. Deleting it is
safe: holds fall back to the pre-v1.5.2 rule until it is rebuilt.
.TP
.I /etc/nog/sources.toml
Source kill-switch state, managed by
.B nog activate
/
.BR "nog deactivate" ;
written via
.BR "sudo tee" .
A missing file or missing key means the source is active. An unreadable file
fails CLOSED — every source is treated as deactivated until the file is
rewritten by an activate/deactivate command.
.TP
.I /etc/pacman.conf
Parsed for enabled repositories. Written only by
.B nog activate/deactivate chaotic-aur
(section toggle with a \fB#nog#\fR marker), always preceded by a timestamped
backup (\fIpacman.conf.nog-bak-<stamp>\fR).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/nog/logs/
Per-run CSV update logs (\fBYYYYMMDD nog-update.csv\fR), one row per pending
package per run with its source, its own outcome and, when a step did not
complete, the reason; plus reboot advice (\fBYYYYMMDD nog-reboot.csv\fR); and,
since v1.5.8, one line per nog run of any command
(\fBYYYYMMDD nog-runs.csv\fR: date, time, user, command, status, outcome).
Since v1.6.0, each run from a terminal is also kept whole, as it appeared on
screen (\fBYYYYMMDD-HHMMSS\fR \fIcommand\fR\fB.log\fR), recorded with
.BR script (1)
(output only: nothing typed is kept); these are deleted after 30 days.
Not recorded: \fB--json\fR runs, runs without a terminal, or without
\fBscript\fR installed.
A recorded run names only its \fB.log\fR when it ends; the CSV logs are nog's
own bookkeeping (the hold clock reads the update log).
Every run ends by naming the logs it wrote.
Columns since v1.4.3:
.nf
.B "  date,time,user,source,bucket,package,"
.B "  old_version,new_version,tier,note,outcome,detail"
.fi
Earlier files keep their original ten-column header; on the day of the upgrade
the new header follows a blank line in the same file. Location configurable via
.B [paths] run_logs
in
.IR nog.conf ;
files older than 90 days are pruned after each run.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/nog
The nog binary.
.
.SH TIER SYSTEM
Every package nog manages is classified into one of three tiers, defined in
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml .
Each tier has a hold window (from the
.B [holds]
section of
.IR /etc/nog/nog.conf )
that must elapse after the package's upstream publish date before the update is
applied during
.BR "nog update" .
.PP
The keyring packages \fBarchlinux-keyring\fR and \fBchaotic-keyring\fR are
never held (since v1.5.6): holding them back would make every later signature
check fail. They are installed in their own step before the rest of the update.
If pacman's key store was never set up, nog runs \fBpacman-key --init\fR and
\fB--populate\fR first.
.PP
Since v1.5.2 the window is counted from the \fBfirst\fR new version seen for the
installed version, not from the newest build: Arch carries only the newest build,
and a new build every week or two used to restart the countdown indefinitely
(issue #27). First sightings are kept in the hold record (see
.BR FILES ).
When the window is over, the newest version is released once it is itself at
least the tier's \fBsafety wait\fR old: \fBtier1_safety_days\fR (default 7),
\fBtier2_safety_days\fR (3) and \fBtier3_safety_days\fR (1) under
.BR [holds] .
Rows show \fBwaiting since <date> \(pc N newer versions skipped\fR, and
\fBnewest build too new\fR during the safety wait.
.SS Tier 1 \(em 30-day hold, auto-release by default
The most critical packages: kernel, bootloader, glibc, systemd, mesa. By default
(\fBmanual_signoff = false\fR), Tier 1 auto-releases after a 30-day hold window
\(em a full month of community testing before an update lands on your system.
.PP
Expert mode: set
.B manual_signoff = true
under
.B [tier1]
in
.IR tier-pins.toml
to switch Tier 1 to always-held; updates release only via
.B "nog unlock <pkg> --promote" .
Recommended only for users who want to review every kernel / glibc / systemd
upgrade personally.
.PP
Default Tier 1 packages:
.BR linux ", " linux-zen ", " linux-lts ", " linux-hardened ", " systemd ", "
.BR systemd-libs ", " glibc ", " grub ", " efibootmgr ", " mkinitcpio ", "
.BR pacman ", " mesa .
.SS Tier 2 \(em 15-day hold
Key system services, and whatever you pin there. Held for 15 days after upstream
publish \(em enough time for major regressions to surface without falling far behind.
.PP
Default Tier 2 packages:
.BR pipewire ", " pipewire-pulse ", " wireplumber ", " networkmanager :
generic plumbing (trimmed in v2.1.0). A desktop and its applications are a
distribution's or the user's choice to pin; KognogOS ships its own, fuller list.
.SS Tier 3 \(em 7-day hold
Everything else. A short 7-day safety buffer without meaningful delay. Anything
not listed in Tier 1 or Tier 2 falls into Tier 3 automatically.
.SS Coupling \(em \fI<X>-headers\fR follows its kernel
A kernel package and its matching
.IR <name>-headers
package share a single PKGBUILD upstream, which means they share a single
build date and must be installed at the same version on disk \(em every DKMS
hook (e.g.
.BR nvidia-open-dkms (1))
rebuilds against
.IR <kernel>-headers
and places its output under
.IR /usr/lib/modules/<KVER>/ ,
which is only present when
.IR <kernel>
itself is installed at the matching version.
.PP
.B nog
auto-couples this relationship: any package matching the
.IR <X>-headers
pattern is classified as Tier 1 when
.IR <X>
is Tier 1. This is hardcoded behavior, not configurable. The Arch naming
convention is universal and the failure mode (broken DKMS module, unbound
GPU after reboot) is severe enough that opting out is not supported.
.SS Linchpins: glibc and gcc-libs (v2.0.0, issue #5)
A fast-lane package is compiled against the foundation of the moment. When
.B glibc
or
.B gcc-libs
has a pending update that changes its \fIseries\fR (glibc 2.44 \(-> 2.45,
which brings new \fBGLIBC_2.45\fR symbol versions; gcc-libs 15 \(-> 16,
new \fBGLIBCXX\fR ones) and that update is held by its Tier 1 window, every
repository package in the Ready bucket that was \fIbuilt after\fR the new
linchpin's build moves to Held with it \(em \fB"12 days \(bu built after the new
glibc, moves with it"\fR \(em because it was probably linked against it and
would fail at runtime (\fBGLIBC_2.45 not found\fR; the 2026-08-04 spectacle
breakage). A same-series rebuild (2.44 \(-> 2.44, most months) cannot add a
symbol version and moves nothing: measured on the test desktop on 2026-10-14,
0 of 160 pending packages. The way out is the one nog already offers: promote
the linchpin (\fBnog update --promote glibc,lib32-glibc\fR), never downgrade
the fast lane.
.SS Interpreters and their module folders (v2.1.0, F-25, issue #61)
.B perl
and
.B python
install their compiled modules into a folder named for the interpreter's
version series:
.I /usr/lib/perl5/5.42/
and
.IR /usr/lib/python3.14/ .
When one of them has a Ready update that changes its series (perl 5.42
\(-> 5.44), every installed package with files under the old folder must move
with it, or its modules sit where the new interpreter never looks. Arch states
this nowhere but in the paths (no versioned dependency, no ABI provide), so nog
reads the installed packages' file lists, only when such a move is pending. A
package that is held lends its countdown (\fB"1 day \(bu coupled to
apparmor"\fR); one with no update at all (not rebuilt yet, or from the AUR)
reads \fBblocked by <package>\fR. A same-series update (5.44.0 \(-> 5.44.1)
moves nothing. Found on 2026-10-23: perl went 5.42 \(-> 5.44 while apparmor
and subversion, already rebuilt for it, were held one more day. The way
through is \fBnog update --promote perl\fR, which brings the held partners
along.
.SS Groups (advanced)
The optional
.B [groups]
table in
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
is the escape hatch for non-standard kernel names whose headers do not match
the
.IR <X>-headers
pattern (e.g.
.IR linux-cachyos-cacule-headers ),
or for bundling extra packages into a kernel's tier.
.PP
.RS
.nf
[groups]
cachyos-bundle = [
    "linux-cachyos",
    "linux-cachyos-headers",
    "linux-cachyos-cacule-headers",
]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every member of a group inherits the highest tier present among any other
member of the same group.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Install a package (nog prompts for sudo itself if needed):
.B nog install htop
.TP
Update the system:
.B nog update
.TP
Search with tier annotations:
.B nog search firefox
.TP
Pin a package to Tier 2:
.B nog pin htop --tier=2
.TP
Force-upgrade a held Tier 1 package:
.B nog unlock linux-zen --promote
.TP
Keep a package where it is, whatever updates come:
.B nog lock electron39
.TP
Everything about one package, from whichever source has it:
.B nog info spotify
.TP
The dashboard, and what nog has done:
.B nog status
.B nog history --days 7
.TP
Clean up every source, picking the categories:
.B nog clean
.
.SH PRIVILEGES AND SUDO
.B nog
itself always runs as the invoking user; it is not designed to be run with
.BR sudo .
If you prefix
.B sudo nog
while an AUR helper is configured, nog detects the invocation (via
.B $SUDO_USER
or
.BR $SUDO_UID )
and exits with an error, because
.BR yay (8)
and
.BR paru (8)
refuse to run as root.
.PP
nog escalates to root in exactly five places, all as transparent
.BR sudo (8)
subprocess invocations:
.TP
.B sudo pacman -S | -U | -R | -Syu ...
For package transactions.
.B "nog update"
always hands the official repositories to
.B "sudo pacman -Syu"
directly (since v1.3.0, one manager per source), and
.B "nog install"
of a package file always uses
.B "sudo pacman -U"
(v1.5.0).
.BR install " by name, " remove " and " "unlock --promote"
use
.B sudo pacman
only when no AUR helper is configured; with a helper, nog hands the
transaction to the helper as the invoking user, and the helper runs its own
.B sudo pacman
internally.
.TP
.B "sudo rm -f -- <cache files> | sudo rm -rf -- <cache>/download-XXXXXX"
Only in
.B "nog clean"
(v1.5.5), only after you answer yes, and only on package files and stale
leftover download folders directly inside pacman's configured cache directories.
.TP
.B "sudo snap refresh --hold=<hours> | sudo snap refresh <names>"
For snap holds and snap refreshes (v1.2.0; the hold step added in v1.4.1).
snapd requires root for both. Nothing else about the snap source does.
.TP
.B "sudo tee /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml | /etc/nog/sources.toml"
For the files
.B nog
writes, during
.BR "nog pin" " and " "nog activate" / "nog deactivate" .
The new contents are rebuilt in memory and piped through
.BR tee ;
nog itself never runs with root privileges.
.TP
.B sudo cp --preserve=all /etc/pacman.conf ...
To take a timestamped backup before editing the Chaotic\-AUR section, and only
during
.BR "nog activate" / "nog deactivate chaotic-aur" .
.PP
Outside that one Chaotic\-AUR section of
.IR /etc/pacman.conf ,
.B nog
modifies no other file on your system \(em not
.IR /etc/pacman.d/ ,
not
.IR /var/lib/pacman/local/ ,
not the pacman keyring, and not
.IR /etc/sudoers .
It is a thin wrapper over
.BR pacman (8),
.BR yay (8),
or
.BR paru (8);
every install, remove, and upgrade is subject to pacman's signature
verification and conflict resolution, which nog cannot bypass.
.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS \fBcheckupdates failed: Cannot find the fakeroot binary\fR
The update check comes from \fBpacman-contrib\fR and needs \fBfakeroot\fR, which
pacman-contrib lists only as optional. nog depends on it since v1.5.7; on an older
install, or if it was removed, install it together with a full update:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo pacman -Syu fakeroot
.RE
.PP
Not \fBpacman -S fakeroot\fR alone: on old package lists that is a partial upgrade.
.SS DKMS rebuild fails after \fBnog update\fR
If a DKMS hook (typically
.BR nvidia-open-dkms ,
.BR nvidia-dkms ,
or
.BR virtualbox-host-dkms )
emits
.RS
.PP
.B "ERROR: Missing <KVER> kernel modules tree for module <name>/<version>."
.RE
.PP
your kernel and its headers have diverged on disk \(em headers are at
.IR <KVER>
but the kernel binary for that version isn't installed under
.IR /usr/lib/modules/<KVER>/ .
.PP
In v1.0.3 and later this state is detected automatically; the desync warning
block in
.B nog update
points at the recovery flag:
.PP
.RS
.B nog update --realign
.RE
.PP
Manual recovery (use when
.B --realign
cannot apply, e.g. headers are ahead of any pending kernel upgrade):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo pacman -S linux-zen linux-lts      # adjust to your kernels
sudo pacman -S nvidia-open-dkms          # or the failing DKMS package
.fi
.RE
.PP
Verify the rebuild with
.B dkms status
and look for the expected
.I .ko.zst
files under
.IR /usr/lib/modules/$(uname\ -r)/updates/dkms/ .
.SS Verifying coupling is active
.B nog search linux-zen-headers
should annotate the result with
.RB [ "Tier 1 \(em 30d hold" ].
A green Tier 3 annotation means you are still running v1.0.2 or earlier;
upgrade nog before the next
.BR "nog update" .
.
.SS \fBinstalling <A> breaks dependency '<lib>.so=N' required by <B>\fR
pacman refuses the whole transaction and nothing installs. A package whose hold
has expired bumps a shared library version, while a package still linking the
old one is inside its window; nog cleared one and held the other, and pacman
cannot split the pair.
.PP
Nothing is broken \(em pacman caught it and declined. Move the pair forward
together with
.BR "nog install <A> <B>" ,
which installs both in one transaction. Never downgrade the cleared package
back; the safe direction is forward, onto the version the repositories already
carry. Waiting also works: the held package releases on its own schedule and
the wall disappears with it.
.PP
As of v1.3.1
.B nog
holds the pair rather than letting you reach this: the row that would have
been Ready moves to Held and names its partner, e.g.
.RB \(dq "1 day \(bu coupled to ffmpeg4.4" \(dq,
and both clear on the same run. When the partner has no pending update at all
\(em a foreign or AUR package built against the old library \(em there is no
countdown to inherit and the note reads
.RB \(dq "blocked by <package>" \(dq,
meaning the hold will not lift on its own: rebuild or update that package, or
move the pair forward with
.B nog install
as above.
.PP
Since v1.4.2 the rule also reads the programs themselves. A package can link
a library without declaring it at the right version \(em chaotic-aur's
.B ffmpeg-obs
declared plain
.B libbluray
while linking
.BR libbluray.so.3 ,
so when
.B libbluray
moved to
.B .so.4
pacman was satisfied and nine packages broke silently. When a pending update
drops a library version,
.B nog
now reads the
.B DT_NEEDED
entries of every installed program and library and treats a real linker
exactly like a declared one. The scan runs only when an update drops a library
version, which is rare, and prints a line saying so. A package that ships its
own copy of the library is not counted as a linker, and a package that ships
the library file on the loader path keeps it available even without declaring
it.
.PP
The raw pacman error is still reachable on v1.3.0 and earlier, or when the
local package database at
.I /var/lib/pacman/local
cannot be read \(em
.B nog
says so and carries on without the rule.
.SS pacman's log shows only one ignored package
.B nog
passes a single comma-joined
.B \-\-ignore
argument. pacman splits that string in place inside its own
.IR argv ,
then writes its
.RB \(dq Running\ '...' \(dq
line, so the log records the first name and drops the rest \(em one name can
mean a hundred and sixty were passed. The holds were applied correctly; the
.RB \(dq "warning: <pkg>: ignoring package upgrade" \(dq
lines are the accurate record. A pacman logging artefact, not a nog defect.
